// beta_firestore.rules , proposed Firestore rules for the beta relay
// ===================================================================
// NOT DEPLOYED. These are written for review and are item H4's to deploy.
// There is deliberately no firebase.json beside them, because the only
// firebase.json in this repo is site/firebase.json and it configures the live
// public website: a deploy that picked up a config from this directory is a
// deploy that could touch Hosting.
//
// What these rules cover is the layout firebase_store.py writes:
//
//     beta/<galaxy>                  the clock, the roster, the hash, the name
//     beta/<galaxy>/archive/<turn>   what the referee recorded for a turn
//
// The worker holds a service account. A service account bypasses security
// rules entirely, so there is no allow rule for the worker anywhere below.
// Everything here describes what a player's launcher may do, and a player's
// launcher never writes a turn.
//
// Three properties, which is what H4 asks for:
//   1. a client may write only its own submission, for the current turn
//   2. a client may never write a published turn
//   3. a client may never delete anything
//
// Property 1 is a Cloud Storage property and is in beta_storage.rules, where
// there is also a note about the part of it the current layout cannot express.
//
// Two things these rules do NOT do, named here rather than discovered later.
//
// **The roster is readable.** The galaxy document carries `civs`, so any
// authenticated client that reads the row reads who is playing. J4 says a
// refused name must not name the other players, and that is not achievable
// while the roster is a field of a document clients read. The launcher reads
// it today, at release/launcher.py:1461, to decide roster_problem. Closing
// this means either moving the roster to a document clients cannot read and
// answering the seat question through a per-player document, or accepting that
// the roster is public. It is a J4 decision and not a rules bug.
//
// **This is not an anti-cheat measure.** Cheating is an accepted property of
// this beta. These rules exist because a store with no rules can be emptied by
// anyone who finds it, and because upload and egress are metered.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // Anything not named below is denied, which is the default and is left as
    // the default rather than restated, so that a path added later is denied
    // until someone decides otherwise.

    match /beta/{galaxy} {
      // Every launcher reads this: it is the clock, the deadline the launcher
      // polls against, and the row the Games tab shows. One document read.
      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      // Only the worker publishes a turn, and the worker is not subject to
      // these rules. No client writes this document for any reason.
      allow write: if false;

      match /archive/{turn} {
        // Readable so that a launcher can show what happened in a turn, and
        // so that K3's abandonment count can be taken from the archive by
        // anything that can read it.
        allow read: if request.auth != null;
        allow write: if false;
      }
    }

    // J3 will add a join request path under beta/<galaxy>, written by the
    // client and applied by the worker at a turn boundary. It is not written
    // here because its shape is not decided, and a rule that guesses at a
    // schema is a rule that will be wrong. It will need: create and update by
    // the owning uid only, no delete, and a size cap on the claimed name.
  }
}
